Tap the screen to focus, with the rear camera's motor

WHAT IT DOES
------------
A tap on the viewfinder focuses on that point. A reticle appears: white while it
searches, green if it succeeded, red if it did not. Saying that focus was NOT
achieved matters as much as the other case -on a scene without edges the search
cannot converge, and keeping quiet about it would leave the user waiting for
something that is not going to happen-.

WHY HERE AND NOT IN LIBCAMERA
-----------------------------
Because the simple pipeline handler does not publish AfMode or LensPosition for
this sensor, and because this phone's actuator has two constraints that no
generic autofocus contemplates:

  - it only accepts commands WHILE THE SENSOR IS STREAMING, because the CCI bus
    lives with the sensor;
  - and on closing its node, the driver returns the lens to the origin and
    powers the chip down.

The second one is solved by the `enfoque-surya` service (camara/enfoque-surya),
which keeps the node open and accepts positions through /run/enfoque-surya. The
first one stops being a problem right here: the preview IS ALREADY streaming
when the user taps the screen. Which is, looking at it properly, how any real
autofocus works.

THE METHOD, AND WHAT WAS VALIDATED BEFORE WRITING IT
-----------------------------------------------------
Climbing the sharpness hill: five positions spread over the whole travel and two
refinements of five around the best one. It was tried first from the PC, with
the controls tool, and it gave 512 -the same value as an exhaustive sweep of
nine positions by another route-:

     position  sharpness
        0        4.15
      398        5.52  ###########
      498        8.01  ################################
      512        9.37  ############################################
      569        6.96  #######################
     1023        4.23

Here it comes out much cheaper: in the preview the frames arrive every ~50 ms
instead of every 1.2 s.

Sharpness is the variance of the horizontal second difference, normalised by the
mean level squared. Normalising is not cosmetic: raising the gain raises the
signal and its noise at the same time, and without normalising a change of
exposure halfway through the search would read as a change of focus.

THREE THINGS THAT LOOK LIKE DETAILS AND ARE NOT
------------------------------------------------
1. FOUR FRAMES ARE THROWN AWAY after moving the lens. The actuator takes time to
   get there and the camera pipeline runs with a lag. Measuring without waiting
   does not give a noisier curve: it gives a SHIFTED curve, which is worse
   because it looks good.

2. THE POINT IS TRANSLATED. The preview is rotated in software before being
   painted -the sensor is mounted crooked, 270 degrees on the rear camera-, so
   the point the user taps is not the point of the image that reaches the meter.
   And with the zoom applied it does not match either: the viewfinder is
   magnified from the centre, that is, a tap at the edge falls much further in.
   Without both corrections, the camera would focus somewhere other than where
   it is tapped.

3. IF THE HILL IS FLAT, THE LENS IS NOT TOUCHED. A dark scene measures sensor
   noise, and noise does NOT go out of focus because it is added after the
   optics: the search converges all the same and returns a number that looks
   every bit as good. It really happened -a whole search gave 972 with the
   camera looking at nothing-. Sharpness is required to vary by at least x1.25;
   if it does not, the focus is left where it was and the reticle comes out red.

And the write to the pipe goes with O_NONBLOCK: opening a pipe for writing
BLOCKS until somebody is reading, and this runs on the interface thread. Without
it, not having the service installed would hang the whole application.

LESS NOISE: EACH STAGE IS WALKED TOWARDS THE NEAREST SIDE
-----------------------------------------------------------
A VCM is a coil that pushes the lens against a spring, and every long jump is
AUDIBLE. Trying it on the phone: "you can hear some clicking".

The first version always asked for the positions in increasing order, without
looking at where the lens had been left, so between one stage and the next it
made a long trip for nothing:

    coarse:  0 -> 256 -> 512 -> 767 -> 1023    (ends right at the top)
    fine:    1023 -> 256 -> 384 -> ...         (767 back, useless)

Now each stage is walked towards the side that starts closest to where the lens
is. The SAME positions are measured and the SAME result comes out: only the
order changes, and with it, the noise and the time.

